From 81f96fa8f275bb9469bcac5c9013a01e6f112aff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 22:25:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/94] cpufreq: qcom-hw: let the genpd core bind the CPRh
 required-opp device

Since __genpd_dev_pm_attach() learned to call genpd_set_required_opp_dev(),
a consumer with a single power domain whose operating points name
required-opps gets dev_pm_opp_set_config() run on it by the genpd core
during the attach.  A CPU here is exactly that, so asking for the same
thing through PD_FLAG_REQUIRED_OPP configures the table a second time and
takes a second reference on it, for a binding that is already in place.

The core also does it at the better moment, before the operating points
are added, and owns the token, so it is released again on detach.

This is not the attach hang.  The core performs that binding whether or
not the flag is passed, so removing it changes what this driver asks for,
not what happens.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c | 12 ++++++------
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
index 951f271ac..a4408de49 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
@@ -540,11 +540,11 @@ static int qcom_cpufreq_gen_params(struct device *cpu_dev,
 {
 	struct dev_pm_domain_list *cpr_pd_list = NULL;
 	/*
-	 * PD_FLAG_REQUIRED_OPP makes the OPP core create the CPU's OPP table
-	 * and bind it to the CPRh domain that provides its required-opps.
-	 * It must run while that table is still empty, which is why the CPRh
-	 * corner setup is deferred to qcom_cpr3_cprh_init() below instead of
-	 * happening in the domain's attach_dev callback.
+	 * No PD_FLAG_REQUIRED_OPP: the genpd core already binds the CPU as the
+	 * required-opp consumer of this domain while attaching, because the CPU
+	 * has a single power domain and its operating points name required-opps.
+	 * Asking for it here only repeats that work on a table the core has
+	 * already configured.
 	 *
 	 * PD_FLAG_NO_DEV_LINK because the consumer here is a CPU device, which
 	 * never probes and never runtime suspends, so a device link to the
@@ -555,7 +555,7 @@ static int qcom_cpufreq_gen_params(struct device *cpu_dev,
 	struct dev_pm_domain_attach_data attach_data = {
 		.pd_names = cprh_genpd_names,
 		.num_pd_names = 1,
-		.pd_flags = PD_FLAG_NO_DEV_LINK | PD_FLAG_REQUIRED_OPP,
+		.pd_flags = PD_FLAG_NO_DEV_LINK,
 	};
 	struct platform_device *pdev = cpufreq_get_driver_data();
 	const struct qcom_cpufreq_soc_data *soc_data = qcom_cpufreq.soc_data;
